$ # PR #714 — peasant web stop must only signal its own web server (issue #671)
$ # A stale web-<port>.pid can name ANY reused PID. Plant a harmless
$ # stand-in (sleep 300, started by this script) in a fake PID file.

================ BEFORE: develop (be2bc57f) ================
$ sleep 300 &  # stand-in PID 50857, planted in web-18693.pid
    PID COMMAND         COMMAND
  50857 sleep           sleep 300
$ peasant-before web stop --port 18693
HTTP shutdown failed: Post "http://localhost:18693/api/v1/shutdown": dial tcp [::1]:18693: connect: connection refused, falling back to SIGTERM
Sent SIGTERM to PID 50857
exit=0
>>> stand-in 50857 was KILLED by web stop on develop (BUG, #671)

================ AFTER: PR #714 head (47b14407, rebased) ================
$ sleep 300 &  # stand-in PID 50896, planted in web-18694.pid
    PID COMMAND         COMMAND
  50896 sleep           sleep 300
$ peasant-after web stop --port 18694
HTTP shutdown failed: Post "http://localhost:18694/api/v1/shutdown": dial tcp [::1]:18694: connect: connection refused, falling back to SIGTERM
Error: stale PID file /home/hatch/workspace/pr-evidence/pr-714/evidence/demo-scratch/after-home/.local/state/peasant/web-18694.pid: PID 50896 is not a peasant web server on port 18694, so nothing was signalled; the file was removed. If a server is still running, find it with your process list and stop it there
exit=1
>>> stand-in 50896 is STILL ALIVE — nothing was signalled (FIXED)
$ ls $XDG_STATE_HOME/peasant/  # stale PID file was removed
# now killing my own stand-in myself:
stand-in cleaned up

================ LEGIT PATH: real server start/stop (PR binary) ================
$ peasant-after web start --no-browser --port 18695
